+## Test results
+
+`cargo test -p ciborium --no-fail-fast` (rustc 1.95.0):
+
+- unittests, codec (251), error (105), fuzz, macro (313), no_std, recursion, tag, doc tests: **all pass**.
+- canonical: **11 pass, 1 fails** — the deliberately pinned
+  `props::known_failure_cmp_value_tag_pairs_ignore_length` (see below). The
+  failure is deterministic: its generator only produces inputs in the buggy
+  region, so it fails on every run/seed.
+
+Verification steps performed per the skill:
+
+- **Liveness**: temporarily inverted the assertion in `value_bytes_roundtrip`;
+  hegel failed and shrank to the minimal counterexample `Null`. Reverted, and
+  the `.hegel/` failure database was removed afterwards.
+- **Exploratory 10x run**: all hegel tests were run once with
+  `test_cases = 1000` (10x the default). No additional failures were found.
+  Committed tests use the default count.
+
+Unrelated pre-existing issue: `cargo clippy -p ciborium --tests` fails with
+`clippy::multiple_crate_versions` (`syn` 2.0.119 via `rstest_macros` vs
+`syn` 3.0.3 via `serde_derive` 1.0.229) because `src/lib.rs` has
+`#![deny(clippy::cargo)]`. This is triggered by any fresh `Cargo.lock`
+resolution with current serde and is **not** caused by hegeltest — verified
+by re-running clippy with `hegeltest` removed from the dependency graph
+(same failure). With that lint set to `warn`, clippy reports no issues in
+the new test code.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### `cmp_value` orders tag-vs-tag by tag number, violating its documented byte order and breaking `Ord` transitivity
+
+- **Pinned by**: `ciborium/tests/canonical.rs::props::known_failure_cmp_value_tag_pairs_ignore_length`
+- **Minimal counterexample** (hegel-shrunk):
+  `cmp_value(Tag(0, Bytes([0; 10])), Tag(24, Null))` returns `Less`, but the
+  encodings are 12 bytes (`c0 4a 00…`) vs 3 bytes (`d8 18 f6`), so the
+  documented rule ("the shorter one sorts earlier") requires `Greater`.
+- **Code path**: `ciborium/src/value/canonical.rs`, `cmp_value`, the
+  `(Tag(t, v), Tag(ot, ov))` arm: it compares `Value::from(*t)` against
+  `Value::from(*ot)` with the *derived* (numeric) `PartialOrd` before ever
+  looking at total serialized length, then compares inner values. Every other
+  arm respects length-first byte order.
+- **Consequence beyond the doc violation**: `CanonicalValue`'s `Ord` becomes
+  intransitive when tags mix with other types, e.g.
+  `A = Tag(0, Bytes([0;10]))` (12 bytes), `B = Tag(24, Null)` (3 bytes),
+  `C = Text("abc")` (4 bytes): the tag rule gives `A < B`, the byte rule gives
+  `B < C` and `C < A`. An intransitive `Ord` can make `sort`/`BTreeMap` on
+  `CanonicalValue` panic ("comparison method violates its contract") or
+  produce nondeterministic canonical map ordering.
+- **Not fixed** per instructions; sibling properties (8, 10, 11, 12) exclude
+  tag-vs-tag comparisons / tag map keys with comments referencing the pinned
+  test.
+
+### Observed but not externally visible: `Integer::canonical_len` returns 17 for `[-2^64, -2^63-1]`
+
+`ciborium/src/value/integer.rs::canonical_len` falls through to the bignum
+branch (`16 + 1`) for negative values whose magnitude needs exactly 64 bits
+(`i64::try_from` and `u64::try_from` both fail), although such integers
+actually serialize to 9 bytes (`0x3b` + 8). Analysis shows the resulting
+*order* still coincides with the byte order (all such integers sort after all
+other integers of real length ≤ 9 in both schemes), so property 8 cannot
+observe it and no test pins it — but the length itself is wrong and is a
+latent hazard if `canonical_len` is ever used for anything other than this
+comparison.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **`Value` -> bytes -> `Value` roundtrip including tags 2/3**: not a sound
+  property — ciborium deliberately decodes wire bignum tags into
+  `Value::Integer` (or a `Value::Tag` with leading-zero-stripped bytes). This
+  is documented behavior (the "Not In RFC" rstest cases in `tests/codec.rs`,
+  e.g. `c2540000…01` decodes to `1`). The roundtrip property excludes tags
+  2/3 via the generator config; the `Value`-serializer identity property
+  (which has no wire step) still covers them.
+- **Derived `PartialEq` for float roundtrips**: `Float(NaN) != Float(NaN)` is
+  a comparison artifact, not a roundtrip failure. Rather than abandoning NaN
+  inputs, the tests compare floats bitwise (`common::eqv`), which is the
+  stronger property (the crate promises bit-lossless float coercion) and it
+  holds.
+- **Byte-order oracle for tag-vs-tag comparisons**: sound as a *specification*
+  but violated by the implementation; retained as the pinned known-failure
+  test rather than abandoned.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The `compose!`-returning-`BoxedGenerator` pattern for recursive data
+  (documented in the Rust reference under `#[hegel::composite]`) worked
+  exactly as described for the recursive `Value` generator, including passing
+  a config struct through the recursion.
+- The documented MSRV interaction (`cargo add` failing against the crate's
+  `rust-version`, retry with `--ignore-rust-version`) was hit verbatim; the
+  reference's Setup note made it a non-event.
+- The absence of a global case-count override (gotcha 13) makes the
+  "exploratory 10x run" step mechanical but clunky across three files — a
+  `HEGEL_TEST_CASES`-style env override would remove a sed-edit/revert cycle.
+- `tests/common/mod.rs` (mentioned in the reference for sharing generators
+  between integration tests) was essential here, since three existing test
+  files each needed the same recursive generator; the skill's "add to
+  existing test files, don't create new ones" rule and the shared-module
+  pattern compose well.
+- The skill's advice to read the docs before asserting a property paid off
+  twice: the "Not In RFC" rstest cases flagged the bignum-tag decode collapse
+  before it could masquerade as a roundtrip bug, and the `cmp_value` doc
+  comment provided the exact oracle that exposed the real tag-ordering bug.
